Track whether there are unsaved changes, and use the browser's `beforeunload` event to warn the user when they try to leave:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export default function NoteEditor({ onSave }) {
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const [savedText, setSavedText] = useState("");
  const isDirty = text !== savedText;

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isDirty) return;

    const handleBeforeUnload = (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      e.returnValue = ""; // required for Chrome to show the prompt
    };

    window.addEventListener("beforeunload", handleBeforeUnload);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("beforeunload", handleBeforeUnload);
  }, [isDirty]);

  const handleSave = () => {
    onSave(text);
    setSavedText(text);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}
        rows={10}
      />
      <button onClick={handleSave} disabled={!isDirty}>
        Save
      </button>
      {isDirty && <p style={{ color: "#b45309" }}>You have unsaved changes</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Notes:
- Browsers show their **own generic message** ("Leave site? Changes you made may not be saved") — custom text isn't allowed anymore.
- The listener is only attached while there are unsaved changes, so it won't nag after saving.
- This covers closing the tab / reloading. If you use React Router, also add its `useBlocker` hook to catch in-app navigation.
